1
00:00:48,006 --> 00:00:54,971
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

2
00:01:00,935 --> 00:01:01,770
You are…

3
00:01:04,147 --> 00:01:05,398
a spy, aren't you?

4
00:01:11,905 --> 00:01:13,114
A spy from the Gugukdan.

5
00:01:17,911 --> 00:01:18,745
Yes.

6
00:01:20,789 --> 00:01:22,248
I am a spy.

7
00:01:23,333 --> 00:01:24,834
So tell them to arrest me now.

8
00:01:26,544 --> 00:01:28,630
Do I look like I'm joking?

9
00:01:29,422 --> 00:01:30,256
Yesterday,

10
00:01:31,091 --> 00:01:32,634
when the bomb went off…

11
00:01:32,717 --> 00:01:34,135
Are you okay?

12
00:01:34,928 --> 00:01:35,845
Now, let's get out.

13
00:01:40,683 --> 00:01:42,227
Why did you save me?

14
00:01:45,396 --> 00:01:46,731
I'm sorry.

15
00:01:49,943 --> 00:01:50,777
That was…

16
00:01:54,572 --> 00:01:56,241
I didn't save you.

17
00:01:57,784 --> 00:01:59,994
I just tripped.

18
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:06,251
You're lying.

19
00:02:09,838 --> 00:02:12,924
Fine, let's say I really am a spy.

20
00:02:13,675 --> 00:02:16,803
Then do you think I'd come right out
and say, "Yes, I'm a spy"?

21
00:02:17,679 --> 00:02:20,390
I don't know what made you think that.

22
00:02:20,473 --> 00:02:21,474
But I'm telling you,

23
00:02:22,183 --> 00:02:23,768
I'm really not a spy.

24
00:02:25,436 --> 00:02:28,773
Is this about yesterday's banquet bombing?

25
00:02:28,857 --> 00:02:32,152
They said some mentally disturbed
Japanese man acted alone.

26
00:02:34,529 --> 00:02:35,697
But let's be honest.

27
00:02:36,656 --> 00:02:38,241
It doesn't matter if I'm a spy.

28
00:02:38,324 --> 00:02:41,494
The Government-General
can call me one whenever they like.

29
00:02:42,537 --> 00:02:44,497
So what's the point of asking me?

30
00:02:46,124 --> 00:02:48,668
What is it you want me to be?

31
00:02:50,670 --> 00:02:52,714
Are you hoping I'm not a spy, or…

32
00:02:54,174 --> 00:02:55,425
that I am?

33
00:02:57,677 --> 00:02:58,887
I'm hoping you're a spy.

34
00:03:00,847 --> 00:03:03,516
Please! I'd rather you were a spy.

35
00:03:05,393 --> 00:03:06,227
At least then,

36
00:03:06,769 --> 00:03:10,899
you wouldn't be with Yoo Philip
or have to endure his beatings.

37
00:03:10,982 --> 00:03:12,692
And I wouldn't have to feel…

38
00:03:17,947 --> 00:03:20,074
so confused about you anymore.

39
00:03:30,627 --> 00:03:31,461
Come in.

40
00:03:34,005 --> 00:03:35,423
Ms. Keum.

41
00:03:37,926 --> 00:03:38,885
Mr. Sato.

42
00:03:40,595 --> 00:03:41,971
Congratulations.

43
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:50,396
Excuse me.

44
00:03:58,821 --> 00:04:00,281
Ms. Keum.

45
00:04:02,075 --> 00:04:03,159
Did you see this?

46
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:06,162
INSPECTOR GENERAL SATO'S SON
TO MARRY KIM SEUNG-EOK'S DAUGHTER

47
00:04:07,121 --> 00:04:09,374
I guess it's over for me now.

48
00:04:10,708 --> 00:04:11,584
Dad!

49
00:04:11,668 --> 00:04:12,502
EPISODE 11

50
00:04:16,130 --> 00:04:17,090
Dad.

51
00:04:17,173 --> 00:04:20,176
Good grief, Myung-nam.
You're going to burst my eardrums.

52
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,095
This is your doing, isn't it?

53
00:04:22,845 --> 00:04:24,222
Oh, you finally saw it.

54
00:04:24,722 --> 00:04:27,558
I wish they had printed your photo
a little bigger.

55
00:04:27,642 --> 00:04:29,269
Still, that's a nice photo.

56
00:04:29,352 --> 00:04:33,273
How can I make the front page
when the Government-General was bombed?

57
00:04:33,898 --> 00:04:35,942
We haven't even set a date yet.

58
00:04:36,025 --> 00:04:36,901
Oh, come on.

59
00:04:36,985 --> 00:04:39,320
I know exactly what I'm doing.

60
00:04:40,530 --> 00:04:44,158
Thanks to that headline, people will be
talking about your engagement

61
00:04:44,242 --> 00:04:47,078
even more than they do
about the Government-General bombing.

62
00:04:48,162 --> 00:04:50,540
They need to know that I, Kim Seung-eok,

63
00:04:50,623 --> 00:04:52,959
have the inspector general's back.

64
00:04:53,835 --> 00:04:55,003
Dad.

65
00:04:55,503 --> 00:04:58,339
Everyone makes mistakes

66
00:04:58,423 --> 00:05:00,591
and ends up losing face at some point.

67
00:05:01,467 --> 00:05:03,094
If you help at a time like that,

68
00:05:03,177 --> 00:05:05,179
you can get them on your side
at little cost.

69
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:09,559
Dad, please…

70
00:05:09,642 --> 00:05:13,187
Myung-nam, are you crying
because you're moved by my wisdom?

71
00:05:16,482 --> 00:05:17,317
Yes.

72
00:05:18,109 --> 00:05:19,986
You're really something, Dad.

73
00:05:22,030 --> 00:05:24,324
Good heavens, a face like an angel,
but that temper…

74
00:05:24,407 --> 00:05:26,409
What do I do about that temper of hers?

75
00:05:27,076 --> 00:05:30,455
She won't change until her husband
knocks some sense into her.

76
00:05:31,914 --> 00:05:33,541
I can't even…

77
00:05:34,709 --> 00:05:35,918
It came out just fine.

78
00:05:46,512 --> 00:05:47,388
Lee Ga-kyung.

79
00:05:48,181 --> 00:05:49,349
Get a hold of yourself.

80
00:06:03,321 --> 00:06:04,614
Oh, Ms. Yamada.

81
00:06:06,532 --> 00:06:08,618
I thought you'd be
at the Inspector General's Office,

82
00:06:08,701 --> 00:06:10,119
but here you are.

83
00:06:10,203 --> 00:06:12,705
I need to stop by the pneumatic tube room.

84
00:06:15,666 --> 00:06:16,667
Well then.

85
00:06:16,751 --> 00:06:17,585
I'll be off.

86
00:06:17,668 --> 00:06:18,586
Okay.

87
00:06:36,396 --> 00:06:38,398
I can't keep leaving this here.

88
00:06:39,565 --> 00:06:40,441
What should I do?

89
00:06:51,285 --> 00:06:52,120
I'm sorry.

90
00:06:52,203 --> 00:06:54,288
You've got to be kidding me!

91
00:06:54,956 --> 00:06:57,458
I paid a fortune to have this custom-made
at Ginza Boutique!

92
00:06:57,542 --> 00:06:58,835
I'm so sorry.

93
00:06:58,918 --> 00:07:02,213
Who brings smelly food into the building?
What were you thinking?

94
00:07:02,755 --> 00:07:03,673
I'm sorry.

95
00:07:03,756 --> 00:07:05,258
Go wipe it off in the bathroom.

96
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:08,469
This day just keeps getting worse.

97
00:07:09,470 --> 00:07:11,305
Can you send this
through the pneumatic tube?

98
00:07:12,473 --> 00:07:15,685
The inspector general asked me to send it
to the governor-general's residence.

99
00:07:17,186 --> 00:07:18,521
GOVERNOR-GENERAL, IWAMURA SHIGERU

100
00:07:18,604 --> 00:07:21,232
If you hand it to Mr. Mitarai,
he'll send it.

101
00:07:21,816 --> 00:07:22,692
Okay.

102
00:07:23,401 --> 00:07:24,235
Thanks.

103
00:07:26,070 --> 00:07:26,946
Sorry about that.

104
00:07:31,325 --> 00:07:33,911
<i>Naengmyeon </i>doesn't smell, though, right?

105
00:07:35,496 --> 00:07:36,747
I'm a Joseon person.

106
00:07:37,707 --> 00:07:38,541
I see.

107
00:07:38,624 --> 00:07:41,002
Where are you delivering that?

108
00:07:41,544 --> 00:07:42,545
It's for Mr. Toilet.

109
00:07:43,254 --> 00:07:44,088
Toilet…

110
00:07:46,382 --> 00:07:47,467
You mean Mr. Mitarai?

111
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:48,384
Yes.

112
00:07:48,468 --> 00:07:49,927
"MITARAI," "TOILET"
SAME CHARACTERS

113
00:07:50,011 --> 00:07:52,763
Normally, the customer is supposed
to pick it up on the first floor.

114
00:07:53,389 --> 00:07:55,600
But "Toilet" makes me bring it inside.

115
00:07:55,683 --> 00:07:56,767
And that's every day.

116
00:07:56,851 --> 00:07:58,728
Apparently, he can't leave his post.

117
00:07:59,312 --> 00:08:01,022
He never gets tired of <i>naengmyeon.</i>

118
00:08:02,315 --> 00:08:03,191
Every single day?

119
00:08:09,989 --> 00:08:12,658
Operator, put me through
to the governor-general's residence.

120
00:08:14,202 --> 00:08:16,662
Hello, this is the Government-General's
pneumatic tube room.

121
00:08:16,746 --> 00:08:18,164
A pneumatic tube carrier is coming

122
00:08:18,247 --> 00:08:20,458
from the inspector general
to the governor-general,

123
00:08:20,541 --> 00:08:21,667
so please stand by.

124
00:08:21,751 --> 00:08:22,585
Thanks.

125
00:08:25,129 --> 00:08:26,714
Hey, you, <i>naengmyeon yobo.</i>

126
00:08:26,797 --> 00:08:28,799
Stop dawdling and go wait outside!

127
00:08:35,765 --> 00:08:36,807
By the way,

128
00:08:36,891 --> 00:08:40,144
aren't you supposed to pick up
food deliveries at the entrance?

129
00:08:40,228 --> 00:08:42,396
You think I've got
all the time in the world like you?

130
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:44,440
I don't even have time
to take a bathroom break.

131
00:08:44,524 --> 00:08:46,526
It's his job to come pick up the bowls.

132
00:08:46,609 --> 00:08:48,653
Isn't that what <i>yobos </i>are for?

133
00:08:49,445 --> 00:08:51,072
You get lost too!

134
00:08:51,155 --> 00:08:52,114
Seriously.

135
00:09:03,292 --> 00:09:04,168
Go screw yourself.

136
00:09:07,755 --> 00:09:08,881
What did you just say?

137
00:09:13,678 --> 00:09:14,595
Use chopsticks.

138
00:09:14,679 --> 00:09:17,306
In the language of Joseon,
they're called <i>"jeotgarak."</i>

139
00:09:18,057 --> 00:09:19,850
You just cursed at me, didn't you?

140
00:09:21,102 --> 00:09:23,187
You cursed at me in your language!

141
00:09:23,271 --> 00:09:25,606
Good heavens, of course not.

142
00:09:25,690 --> 00:09:27,024
Listen carefully.

143
00:09:27,108 --> 00:09:28,568
The English word is "chopsticks."

144
00:09:28,651 --> 00:09:30,152
In Japanese, it's "<i>hashi</i>."

145
00:09:30,236 --> 00:09:32,113
In the Joseon language,
we say "<i>jeotgarak</i>."

146
00:09:32,905 --> 00:09:33,823
You little…

147
00:09:33,906 --> 00:09:36,826
Your noodles are getting soggy.
You should eat them now.

148
00:09:37,410 --> 00:09:39,912
"<i>Jeotgarak</i>"?

149
00:09:45,293 --> 00:09:47,753
INTERPRETER TRAINEE KEUM SEO-HEE

150
00:09:48,713 --> 00:09:51,132
<i>Since the Gugukdan</i>
<i>was behind yesterday's attack,</i>

151
00:09:52,508 --> 00:09:54,719
<i>could she be one of them too?</i>

152
00:09:56,429 --> 00:09:57,513
Gugukdan…

153
00:10:05,271 --> 00:10:06,772
I just went to the bathroom.

154
00:10:12,528 --> 00:10:15,072
It's pretty dusty.
I need to do some cleaning.

155
00:10:15,156 --> 00:10:15,990
Ms. Keum.

156
00:10:16,073 --> 00:10:16,949
Right.

157
00:10:17,033 --> 00:10:19,660
I'll go check if Mr. Laurent is in yet.

158
00:10:19,744 --> 00:10:20,578
Mr. Laurent

159
00:10:21,245 --> 00:10:23,080
left Gyeongseong early this morning.

160
00:10:23,164 --> 00:10:23,998
What?

161
00:10:29,337 --> 00:10:32,173
Because he was shaken by
what happened yesterday?

162
00:10:33,215 --> 00:10:34,050
Yes.

163
00:10:37,595 --> 00:10:39,263
My father and the chief of police

164
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:43,309
are tracking down a woman
from the Gugukdan.

165
00:10:46,354 --> 00:10:47,688
You should be careful too.

166
00:10:50,733 --> 00:10:51,567
My goodness.

167
00:10:52,151 --> 00:10:53,152
I told you

168
00:10:53,653 --> 00:10:55,696
I'm not a spy.

169
00:10:56,656 --> 00:10:59,283
I don't know why you're telling me this.

170
00:11:04,664 --> 00:11:06,040
Because I want to save you.

171
00:11:12,630 --> 00:11:13,464
So…

172
00:11:16,717 --> 00:11:17,551
stay safe.

173
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:25,476
Okay.

174
00:11:27,144 --> 00:11:28,479
Thanks anyway.

175
00:11:29,271 --> 00:11:30,773
I'm not a spy, though.

176
00:12:39,091 --> 00:12:40,801
<i>You had a high fever all night.</i>

177
00:12:41,677 --> 00:12:44,889
<i>I wish I could stay with you,</i>
<i>but I have to go to work. I'm sorry.</i>

178
00:12:46,056 --> 00:12:48,768
<i>Call me if anything happens.</i>
<i>I'll come right away.</i>

179
00:13:28,432 --> 00:13:29,475
Come to think of it,

180
00:13:30,392 --> 00:13:33,103
how on earth did they get the bomb inside?

181
00:13:33,687 --> 00:13:36,857
Every entrance and passageway
was under guard on the day of the banquet.

182
00:13:37,441 --> 00:13:39,985
It was impossible for outsiders to get in.

183
00:13:42,780 --> 00:13:44,949
Who'd dare bomb the Government-General?

184
00:13:46,534 --> 00:13:47,368
Exactly.

185
00:13:48,869 --> 00:13:50,663
If it <i>was </i>an outsider.

186
00:13:51,622 --> 00:13:52,456
Sorry?

187
00:13:53,082 --> 00:13:53,958
But then,

188
00:13:55,209 --> 00:13:58,254
doesn't a Joseon person go in and out
of the Government-General every day…

189
00:14:04,927 --> 00:14:07,263
riding on an American's coattails?

190
00:14:23,946 --> 00:14:30,411
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

191
00:14:41,672 --> 00:14:42,756
You are…

192
00:14:43,340 --> 00:14:44,508
a spy, aren't you?

193
00:14:46,135 --> 00:14:47,386
<i>I'm hoping you're a spy.</i>

194
00:14:47,887 --> 00:14:49,805
And I wouldn't have to feel…

195
00:14:51,807 --> 00:14:53,726
so confused about you anymore.

196
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:57,813
<i>I saw it myself.</i>

197
00:14:59,064 --> 00:15:00,232
Sato Hideo

198
00:15:01,150 --> 00:15:03,027
murdered Songjeon.

199
00:15:03,986 --> 00:15:05,362
Because I want to save you.

200
00:15:06,697 --> 00:15:09,575
<i>So… stay safe.</i>

201
00:15:16,332 --> 00:15:18,459
None of this adds up.

202
00:15:31,889 --> 00:15:32,723
What the…

203
00:15:33,265 --> 00:15:35,559
Who do you think
you're trying to steal from?

204
00:15:35,643 --> 00:15:36,477
What the hell?

205
00:15:37,394 --> 00:15:39,563
You picked the wrong person to mess with.
I've been…

206
00:15:42,691 --> 00:15:43,525
Wait.

207
00:15:44,652 --> 00:15:45,486
Lee No-ma.

208
00:15:46,070 --> 00:15:47,613
Lee Ga-kyung?

209
00:15:53,994 --> 00:15:55,579
What? The Government-General?

210
00:15:58,832 --> 00:16:01,919
You're telling me
you work for the Government-General?

211
00:16:02,670 --> 00:16:03,504
Yes.

212
00:16:06,799 --> 00:16:11,261
Come on, Ga-kyung, you're just teasing me
because it's been so long.

213
00:16:11,345 --> 00:16:13,055
Come on, let me see your fingers.

214
00:16:13,138 --> 00:16:13,973
You're…

215
00:16:14,723 --> 00:16:15,683
not crossing them.

216
00:16:15,766 --> 00:16:17,226
Seriously? Did you really…

217
00:16:18,727 --> 00:16:20,187
get a job at the Government-General?

218
00:16:22,481 --> 00:16:23,315
Yeah.

219
00:16:28,904 --> 00:16:29,822
Unbelievable.

220
00:16:32,032 --> 00:16:33,117
I know you love money

221
00:16:33,200 --> 00:16:35,703
and couldn't care less
about Joseon's independence,

222
00:16:35,786 --> 00:16:37,746
but how could you work
for the Government-General?

223
00:16:39,248 --> 00:16:42,251
I heard the Gugukdan
bombed the Government-General yesterday.

224
00:16:42,334 --> 00:16:43,252
Bam!

225
00:16:44,086 --> 00:16:45,504
Those Japanese police went…

226
00:16:45,587 --> 00:16:47,923
<i>Bak-chun heard about it and told us.</i>

227
00:16:48,007 --> 00:16:50,384
<i>The whole neighborhood was so thrilled.</i>

228
00:16:50,467 --> 00:16:52,845
You're holding it upside down.

229
00:16:52,928 --> 00:16:54,221
How did you know?

230
00:16:55,347 --> 00:16:57,933
Some people would die
for Joseon's independence.

231
00:16:58,559 --> 00:17:00,436
Ga-kyung, you're a Joseon person too.

232
00:17:00,519 --> 00:17:03,188
And hey, we're friends.

233
00:17:03,272 --> 00:17:05,899
Think about how my parents died
during the Manse Movement,

234
00:17:05,983 --> 00:17:07,443
and about Kkot-nim.

235
00:17:07,526 --> 00:17:10,154
So how can you possibly work for them?

236
00:17:10,237 --> 00:17:11,822
No, you can't.

237
00:17:17,745 --> 00:17:19,913
I'm sorry, No-ma.

238
00:17:25,127 --> 00:17:26,086
Yeah.

239
00:17:27,087 --> 00:17:29,048
Okay. Sure.

240
00:17:30,674 --> 00:17:31,508
I get it.

241
00:17:32,176 --> 00:17:33,218
So, yeah…

242
00:17:35,304 --> 00:17:36,597
Remember your ointment.

243
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:38,140
No-ma, wait.

244
00:17:46,815 --> 00:17:49,443
Kkot-nim was really sick.

245
00:17:49,526 --> 00:17:52,738
She was really sick?

246
00:17:53,906 --> 00:17:55,908
Why? What's wrong? How is she now?

247
00:17:55,991 --> 00:17:58,118
She's a little better now.

248
00:17:58,744 --> 00:18:00,829
But even when she was burning up
and moaning in pain,

249
00:18:02,289 --> 00:18:03,415
she kept asking for you.

250
00:18:04,458 --> 00:18:07,044
I get you couldn't talk about
working for the Government-General,

251
00:18:07,127 --> 00:18:08,962
but how could you not come see Kkot-nim?

252
00:18:10,881 --> 00:18:13,884
Were you trying to cut us
out of your life completely?

253
00:18:32,444 --> 00:18:33,821
There's nothing suspicious.

254
00:18:37,783 --> 00:18:40,536
There are two <i>Josenjings </i>working here.

255
00:18:40,619 --> 00:18:43,372
But there's nothing suspicious here?

256
00:18:43,455 --> 00:18:44,498
Chief.

257
00:18:50,546 --> 00:18:52,506
SIN TAE-JIN

258
00:18:53,257 --> 00:18:54,967
A pawn ticket?

259
00:18:55,551 --> 00:18:56,426
What?

260
00:18:57,052 --> 00:18:58,220
What did you just say?

261
00:19:01,723 --> 00:19:04,309
That I think Sato Hideo

262
00:19:05,811 --> 00:19:07,813
has started to suspect I'm a spy.

263
00:19:07,896 --> 00:19:09,273
But how…

264
00:19:16,488 --> 00:19:18,991
Why? How could you let him find out?

265
00:19:20,117 --> 00:19:20,951
Well…

266
00:19:23,036 --> 00:19:24,246
I'm not sure.

267
00:19:26,582 --> 00:19:28,625
I told him I wasn't a spy, but…

268
00:19:33,755 --> 00:19:35,841
Don't trail off and finish your sentence.

269
00:19:36,425 --> 00:19:38,552
What exactly did Sato Hideo say?

270
00:19:38,635 --> 00:19:41,722
How on earth did he catch on?

271
00:19:42,764 --> 00:19:43,765
That must be why

272
00:19:45,058 --> 00:19:47,769
Sato Hideo came here last night.

273
00:19:47,853 --> 00:19:49,438
He was looking for solid proof.

274
00:19:51,732 --> 00:19:54,776
So, you told him it wasn't true.

275
00:19:54,860 --> 00:19:55,819
What else did you say?

276
00:19:56,653 --> 00:19:58,530
I told him he was seriously mistaken

277
00:19:59,072 --> 00:20:00,657
and that it wasn't true at all.

278
00:20:02,618 --> 00:20:03,452
But…

279
00:20:04,828 --> 00:20:05,704
But?

280
00:20:10,167 --> 00:20:12,794
He said the inspector general
and the chief of police

281
00:20:12,878 --> 00:20:15,422
were after some woman from the Gugukdan.

282
00:20:17,841 --> 00:20:19,218
He warned me to be careful.

283
00:20:19,301 --> 00:20:20,594
That's what Sato Hideo said.

284
00:20:24,431 --> 00:20:26,141
Why would he tell you that?

285
00:20:27,768 --> 00:20:29,353
I'm not sure why he did.

286
00:20:29,937 --> 00:20:32,022
He's trying to win your trust
so he can catch us.

287
00:20:32,105 --> 00:20:32,940
Don't fall for it.

288
00:20:35,400 --> 00:20:36,276
Okay.

289
00:20:39,863 --> 00:20:41,240
Because I want to save you.

290
00:20:43,533 --> 00:20:45,661
You have to get a hold of yourself.

291
00:20:45,744 --> 00:20:47,079
You can't fall for it.

292
00:20:47,579 --> 00:20:48,413
Do you hear me?

293
00:20:50,666 --> 00:20:51,500
Yes.

294
00:20:53,168 --> 00:20:55,170
We need to tell our comrades right away.

295
00:21:13,230 --> 00:21:14,231
Mr. Hideo.

296
00:21:16,233 --> 00:21:17,359
Good evening.

297
00:21:19,111 --> 00:21:20,821
What brings you here at this hour?

298
00:21:21,655 --> 00:21:23,365
I'm sorry to drop by so late.

299
00:21:23,448 --> 00:21:25,534
The police visit yesterday
must've been unpleasant.

300
00:21:25,617 --> 00:21:29,246
I wanted to apologize and thank you
for the article you wrote today.

301
00:21:29,329 --> 00:21:30,455
So I thought I'd drop by.

302
00:21:31,915 --> 00:21:34,001
You didn't have to come all the way here.

303
00:21:34,084 --> 00:21:35,252
In any case, thank you.

304
00:21:37,754 --> 00:21:38,588
All right then.

305
00:21:39,506 --> 00:21:40,424
How about a drink?

306
00:21:42,426 --> 00:21:44,344
Tomorrow happens to be a holiday.

307
00:22:00,235 --> 00:22:03,113
Yanagi. You know how to write
Joseon characters, right?

308
00:22:03,196 --> 00:22:05,073
Write the word "big brother" for me.

309
00:22:17,919 --> 00:22:18,962
BIG BROTHER

310
00:22:23,884 --> 00:22:26,303
FATHER

311
00:22:28,096 --> 00:22:30,265
Does this mean "big brother"
in the Joseon language?

312
00:22:31,516 --> 00:22:33,310
This says <i>"oppa."</i>

313
00:22:33,393 --> 00:22:35,562
It means "big brother."

314
00:22:35,645 --> 00:22:37,397
This means "father."

315
00:22:37,981 --> 00:22:39,274
<i>Abeoji.</i>

316
00:22:40,108 --> 00:22:41,818
<i>"Oppa."</i>

317
00:22:41,902 --> 00:22:43,445
<i>"Abeoji."</i>

318
00:22:46,656 --> 00:22:48,950
Then, what about "mother"?

319
00:23:01,088 --> 00:23:02,798
<i>"Eomeoni."</i>

320
00:23:03,465 --> 00:23:05,425
So that's what it looks like.

321
00:23:05,509 --> 00:23:07,135
"Mother" in the Joseon letters.

322
00:23:07,928 --> 00:23:08,762
Yanagi.

323
00:23:08,845 --> 00:23:11,848
What did my mother look like?

324
00:23:13,100 --> 00:23:16,228
Not the one from the Sato family
who passed away last year.

325
00:23:16,311 --> 00:23:19,564
I mean the Joseon mother
who gave birth to me.

326
00:23:20,774 --> 00:23:24,027
I don't even have a single picture of her.

327
00:23:27,447 --> 00:23:30,742
Mrs. Sato would always glare at me.

328
00:23:31,618 --> 00:23:34,621
She'd beat me when Father wasn't around.

329
00:23:35,288 --> 00:23:36,832
It hurt so much.

330
00:23:37,332 --> 00:23:39,751
She never said anything to Hideo,

331
00:23:40,335 --> 00:23:42,671
but when he and Father weren't around,

332
00:23:43,755 --> 00:23:47,843
she'd call me a filthy <i>Josenjing.</i>

333
00:23:49,261 --> 00:23:52,889
She was so nasty. No wonder
she was sick right up until she died.

334
00:23:56,852 --> 00:23:57,769
Yanagi.

335
00:23:58,520 --> 00:24:01,106
What was my mother like?

336
00:24:01,189 --> 00:24:03,066
You remember her, don't you?

337
00:24:03,150 --> 00:24:04,234
Was she pretty?

338
00:24:04,317 --> 00:24:05,986
What were her eyes like?

339
00:24:06,069 --> 00:24:07,070
What about her nose?

340
00:24:07,154 --> 00:24:08,155
And her lips?

341
00:24:09,239 --> 00:24:13,660
She was so much prettier
than Mrs. Sato, wasn't she?

342
00:24:38,268 --> 00:24:39,144
Yanagi.

343
00:24:40,520 --> 00:24:43,523
She died giving birth to me.

344
00:24:46,610 --> 00:24:49,654
That's why Father hates me, right?

345
00:24:59,581 --> 00:25:00,916
You're home, sir.

346
00:25:01,875 --> 00:25:03,126
I'm sorry.

347
00:25:04,377 --> 00:25:07,255
I didn't realize you had come home.

348
00:25:07,964 --> 00:25:09,174
Welcome home.

349
00:25:16,681 --> 00:25:17,807
I'll clean it up now.

350
00:25:53,593 --> 00:25:55,679
You can't even look me in the eye.

351
00:25:57,764 --> 00:25:58,682
Are you

352
00:25:59,766 --> 00:26:00,600
scared of me?

353
00:26:01,476 --> 00:26:03,270
I'm sorry, Father.

354
00:26:05,772 --> 00:26:07,190
Your mother…

355
00:26:15,240 --> 00:26:17,534
<i>didn't fear me in the slightest.</i>

356
00:26:20,579 --> 00:26:21,746
I'm sorry.

357
00:26:21,830 --> 00:26:24,207
I won't do it again, Father.

358
00:26:26,751 --> 00:26:27,627
Yanagi.

359
00:26:29,004 --> 00:26:31,172
I'm sorry, sir.

360
00:26:31,256 --> 00:26:33,174
I shouldn't have done that.

361
00:26:34,009 --> 00:26:34,884
Use this

362
00:26:36,344 --> 00:26:37,470
to start the fire.

363
00:26:38,638 --> 00:26:41,099
Yes, as you wish.

364
00:26:49,482 --> 00:26:50,317
Where's Hideo?

365
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:53,361
He hasn't come home yet.

366
00:27:24,351 --> 00:27:26,102
Seo-hee brought some chocolate.

367
00:27:27,562 --> 00:27:30,023
Hope you have a good time.

368
00:27:31,149 --> 00:27:31,983
Are you leaving?

369
00:27:34,361 --> 00:27:36,613
Come have a seat.
You already know each other.

370
00:27:41,660 --> 00:27:42,535
Sure.

371
00:27:57,676 --> 00:27:59,636
My wife is taking a bath at the moment.

372
00:28:00,220 --> 00:28:02,847
Seo-hee and I were about to enjoy
some time together.

373
00:28:02,931 --> 00:28:04,182
You've ruined the moment.

374
00:28:07,477 --> 00:28:08,311
Oh dear.

375
00:28:09,646 --> 00:28:11,356
Looks like I'm an unwelcome guest.

376
00:28:13,733 --> 00:28:15,151
But since I'm already here,

377
00:28:15,735 --> 00:28:17,362
you may as well keep up the act.

378
00:28:28,039 --> 00:28:31,418
I heard you asked Seo-hee today
if she was a spy.

379
00:28:32,669 --> 00:28:33,503
Yes.

380
00:28:36,423 --> 00:28:39,467
She may have sticky fingers,
but she's hardly a spy.

381
00:28:41,010 --> 00:28:42,971
She's just a hopeless romantic.

382
00:28:47,892 --> 00:28:49,144
A romantic, you say…

383
00:28:51,354 --> 00:28:54,149
You mean people
who'd risk their lives for love?

384
00:28:55,066 --> 00:28:55,900
Exactly.

385
00:29:02,115 --> 00:29:05,285
Some people would put their lives
on the line for other things.

386
00:29:06,578 --> 00:29:07,495
For example…

387
00:29:10,373 --> 00:29:12,834
the independence of Joseon
or something like that.

388
00:29:18,923 --> 00:29:20,383
The independence of Joseon?

389
00:29:22,177 --> 00:29:24,137
Just as some people would die for love,

390
00:29:25,180 --> 00:29:27,932
others would risk their lives
for Joseon's independence.

391
00:29:31,478 --> 00:29:33,104
What do you think drives them?

392
00:29:34,814 --> 00:29:36,274
What could possibly make them

393
00:29:36,858 --> 00:29:38,485
go that far?

394
00:29:39,486 --> 00:29:41,571
They might never even live
to see independence.

395
00:29:41,654 --> 00:29:42,906
And even if it comes,

396
00:29:43,782 --> 00:29:46,034
what's the point if they're already dead?

397
00:29:52,040 --> 00:29:56,377
Of everyone I've met, no one believes
in love as deeply as you do.

398
00:29:57,337 --> 00:29:58,963
I just started wondering

399
00:29:59,589 --> 00:30:01,216
how you feel about those people.

400
00:30:06,679 --> 00:30:07,514
I'm not sure.

401
00:30:09,265 --> 00:30:12,227
I'm an American,
born and raised in Hawaii,

402
00:30:12,310 --> 00:30:15,063
so I can't fully understand how they feel.

403
00:30:17,732 --> 00:30:20,360
But the Joseon laborers
in Hawaii's sugar plantations

404
00:30:20,443 --> 00:30:22,987
teach their children
to speak the Joseon language,

405
00:30:23,071 --> 00:30:26,282
knowing their kids might never
even visit their homeland.

406
00:30:28,159 --> 00:30:32,288
And they scrape together every penny
to send money to the independence cause.

407
00:30:32,372 --> 00:30:34,958
All while they can't afford
to put butter on their kids' bread.

408
00:30:36,835 --> 00:30:38,419
They're a rather foolish bunch.

409
00:30:41,256 --> 00:30:43,675
Every time I asked,
they all said the same thing.

410
00:30:44,467 --> 00:30:46,386
"Because I love my country."

411
00:30:46,469 --> 00:30:47,387
That was all.

412
00:30:49,722 --> 00:30:51,140
Wouldn't it be scary?

413
00:30:52,892 --> 00:30:53,726
To love…

414
00:30:56,604 --> 00:30:58,064
To love something that much.

415
00:31:05,029 --> 00:31:07,574
One Joseon man in Hawaii
once told me this.

416
00:31:07,657 --> 00:31:09,951
There's nothing frightening
about loving someone.

417
00:31:15,999 --> 00:31:20,086
What's truly frightening is losing them
and having to keep living.

418
00:31:29,345 --> 00:31:31,389
I hope you find a love like that

419
00:31:31,890 --> 00:31:34,601
for yourself someday, Mr. Hideo.

420
00:31:34,684 --> 00:31:37,812
I meant no offense,
especially with your wedding coming up.

421
00:31:37,896 --> 00:31:39,689
I saw the article about your engagement.

422
00:31:39,772 --> 00:31:41,482
It was all over the front page.

423
00:31:44,193 --> 00:31:45,069
I also saw…

424
00:31:46,446 --> 00:31:48,907
the article you wrote
about the banquet yesterday.

425
00:31:50,909 --> 00:31:53,369
I came to thank you for that,
but I nearly forgot.

426
00:31:54,037 --> 00:31:54,871
Thank you.

427
00:31:56,748 --> 00:31:57,665
Much obliged.

428
00:32:04,464 --> 00:32:05,340
Well then,

429
00:32:06,633 --> 00:32:07,759
I'd better get going.

430
00:32:26,861 --> 00:32:28,780
Do you believe people can change?

431
00:32:30,490 --> 00:32:31,574
Even for…

432
00:32:34,535 --> 00:32:38,081
someone like me
who's never felt anything like that?

433
00:32:40,208 --> 00:32:41,084
Absolutely.

434
00:32:43,211 --> 00:32:45,630
Don't we all dream of that kind of love?

435
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:49,801
Ms. Keum…

436
00:32:51,427 --> 00:32:52,679
do you think so too?

437
00:33:04,148 --> 00:33:05,024
Yes.

438
00:33:06,901 --> 00:33:10,446
I think people can change.

439
00:33:18,287 --> 00:33:19,414
Well, Mr. Hideo.

440
00:33:20,289 --> 00:33:22,083
You're the inspector general's son,

441
00:33:22,166 --> 00:33:24,210
yet you want to understand those people.

442
00:33:25,169 --> 00:33:27,130
That's incredibly admirable.

443
00:33:28,423 --> 00:33:32,093
Even so, in their eyes,
you're nothing more than Japan's lapdog.

444
00:33:40,143 --> 00:33:42,270
I apologize if I've offended you.

445
00:33:42,353 --> 00:33:44,313
Perhaps I've had one too many.

446
00:34:00,163 --> 00:34:00,997
What?

447
00:34:01,581 --> 00:34:03,249
There's no Jongno Pawnshop in Jongno?

448
00:34:03,332 --> 00:34:04,167
No, sir.

449
00:34:04,250 --> 00:34:05,752
There were similar names

450
00:34:05,835 --> 00:34:08,755
like "The First Jongno Pawnshop"
or "Jungno Pawnshop."

451
00:34:08,838 --> 00:34:11,549
There were over 20 pawnshops
with similar names.

452
00:34:11,632 --> 00:34:14,052
But none of them was called
"Jongno Pawnshop."

453
00:34:14,135 --> 00:34:17,555
Not only does it not exist today,
but it didn't exist in 1919 either,

454
00:34:17,638 --> 00:34:19,640
the year indicated on the pawn ticket.

455
00:34:19,724 --> 00:34:21,100
What about Sin Tae-jin?

456
00:34:23,436 --> 00:34:26,064
Don't tell me
you couldn't find that name either.

457
00:34:26,147 --> 00:34:26,981
That's right.

458
00:34:27,482 --> 00:34:29,692
The name Sin Tae-jin
didn't appear on the wanted list

459
00:34:29,776 --> 00:34:32,070
for independence fighters
or in any prison records.

460
00:34:32,153 --> 00:34:35,198
And we couldn't find anyone
who knew Interpreter Sato

461
00:34:35,281 --> 00:34:36,240
by his Joseon name.

462
00:34:40,745 --> 00:34:42,205
Jongno Pawnshop…

463
00:34:42,872 --> 00:34:44,123
Sin Tae-jin…

464
00:34:56,302 --> 00:34:57,678
<i>What did you just say?</i>

465
00:34:57,762 --> 00:34:59,597
Her cover's been blown?

466
00:35:00,932 --> 00:35:03,893
Yes, apparently,
Sato Hideo confronted her directly.

467
00:35:03,976 --> 00:35:05,144
Though she denied it.

468
00:35:05,770 --> 00:35:07,522
Sato Hideo…

469
00:35:08,106 --> 00:35:11,400
As if killing Songjeon weren't enough,
now he's up to something.

470
00:35:12,110 --> 00:35:14,070
First, we need to get her out of there.

471
00:35:14,153 --> 00:35:16,531
We can't let her stay
at the Government-General.

472
00:35:16,614 --> 00:35:18,366
But if she quits,

473
00:35:19,075 --> 00:35:20,952
won't they take it as proof
that she's a spy?

474
00:35:21,035 --> 00:35:24,247
Even so, we can't just do nothing
when her cover's already blown.

475
00:35:24,330 --> 00:35:27,667
There's also a chance
Sato Hideo's turned her against us.

476
00:35:27,750 --> 00:35:31,045
We got her to spy for us with money
and a chance to go to America.

477
00:35:31,129 --> 00:35:32,630
She might have accepted a better deal

478
00:35:32,713 --> 00:35:34,966
and started setting a trap for us.

479
00:35:35,049 --> 00:35:36,175
That's enough.

480
00:35:36,259 --> 00:35:38,010
We can't make baseless assumptions.

481
00:35:40,555 --> 00:35:42,515
We'll say our affair is over,

482
00:35:42,598 --> 00:35:44,183
fire her, and send her to Shanghai.

483
00:35:45,351 --> 00:35:46,185
Shanghai?

484
00:35:47,228 --> 00:35:50,022
She's been dreaming of going to America.
Won't that cause trouble?

485
00:35:50,106 --> 00:35:53,442
Then wouldn't it be better
to get rid of her?

486
00:35:54,026 --> 00:35:55,111
Chi-gun!

487
00:35:55,194 --> 00:35:57,905
Should she get caught
on the way to Shanghai and talk,

488
00:35:57,989 --> 00:35:59,532
it'll be the end of all of us.

489
00:35:59,615 --> 00:36:02,785
However strong someone's will is,
torture can break anyone.

490
00:36:02,869 --> 00:36:03,744
Even so…

491
00:36:03,828 --> 00:36:05,163
Yes, I know.

492
00:36:05,246 --> 00:36:08,958
Cheol-yong endured Sato's torture
at Jongno Police Station

493
00:36:09,876 --> 00:36:11,586
and ten years in Seodaemun Prison.

494
00:36:13,129 --> 00:36:15,882
The difference is,
he believed in Joseon's independence.

495
00:36:17,341 --> 00:36:19,302
That girl has no such belief, does she?

496
00:36:21,971 --> 00:36:22,805
Fine.

497
00:36:25,558 --> 00:36:28,644
So in the end, a spy is doomed to die.

498
00:36:28,728 --> 00:36:31,063
They either get caught and killed
or we kill them.

499
00:36:36,235 --> 00:36:37,612
Since when have we treated

500
00:36:37,695 --> 00:36:40,072
the lives of innocent
Joseon people so lightly?

501
00:36:40,907 --> 00:36:43,576
And remember, she trusted us
and worked for our cause.

502
00:36:44,202 --> 00:36:46,329
She gave up her dream of going to America,

503
00:36:46,412 --> 00:36:48,497
and came back to save our lives.

504
00:36:48,581 --> 00:36:52,168
But all our lives are on the line.

505
00:36:52,251 --> 00:36:56,047
Then let's ask someone who's served
our cause as a spy for a long time.

506
00:36:56,130 --> 00:36:57,006
Suzuki.

507
00:36:58,674 --> 00:36:59,926
I mean, Seog-won.

508
00:37:04,305 --> 00:37:07,308
Thirteen years ago,
when I was studying in Tokyo,

509
00:37:07,391 --> 00:37:11,354
I accepted Un-hak's offer to become a spy
and joined the Gugukdan.

510
00:37:12,521 --> 00:37:14,440
I was willing to risk my life then,

511
00:37:15,775 --> 00:37:16,859
and I still am.

512
00:37:19,403 --> 00:37:20,279
But…

513
00:37:21,948 --> 00:37:23,074
she's still young.

514
00:37:23,824 --> 00:37:26,327
I want to make sure
she gets out of this safely.

515
00:37:30,873 --> 00:37:32,833
Yes, understood.

516
00:37:33,668 --> 00:37:36,128
And I'm sorry for what I said. I meant…

517
00:37:36,212 --> 00:37:38,464
I understand. It's all right.

518
00:37:41,175 --> 00:37:43,427
I'll come up with the money
to keep her quiet.

519
00:37:44,095 --> 00:37:45,805
We owe you yet another favor.

520
00:37:45,888 --> 00:37:47,556
You're all risking your lives.

521
00:37:48,474 --> 00:37:50,518
Selling a few pieces of gold is nothing.

522
00:37:52,186 --> 00:37:53,396
We should all lie low

523
00:37:54,021 --> 00:37:55,356
for the time being.

524
00:37:55,439 --> 00:37:57,525
We can't keep hiding forever.

525
00:37:59,318 --> 00:38:01,362
We need to get rid of Sato Hideo.

526
00:38:09,078 --> 00:38:10,621
Kkot-nim.

527
00:38:10,705 --> 00:38:13,958
Ga-kyung, I missed you so much.

528
00:38:14,792 --> 00:38:16,794
Have you been well, Kkot-nim?

529
00:38:17,670 --> 00:38:19,255
Oh dear, I heard you were sick.

530
00:38:19,964 --> 00:38:21,299
You're skin and bones now.

531
00:38:21,799 --> 00:38:23,134
I'm okay.

532
00:38:23,217 --> 00:38:24,552
Are you back from America?

533
00:38:24,635 --> 00:38:26,512
Are you going to live with us again?

534
00:38:27,513 --> 00:38:28,639
No.

535
00:38:28,723 --> 00:38:30,891
I have to go back to America.

536
00:38:32,351 --> 00:38:35,646
Look what I got for you, Kkot-nim.

537
00:38:37,481 --> 00:38:39,483
Ta-da! Chocolate.

538
00:38:39,567 --> 00:38:41,193
Wow, chocolate!

539
00:38:41,277 --> 00:38:42,945
Did you get this in America?

540
00:38:43,029 --> 00:38:45,948
I sure did. I bought it in America.

541
00:38:47,241 --> 00:38:51,662
America? More like
with your Government-General paycheck.

542
00:38:51,746 --> 00:38:53,414
What? What general?

543
00:38:53,998 --> 00:38:55,041
- Kkot-nim.
- Never mind.

544
00:38:55,124 --> 00:38:56,125
Chocolate.

545
00:38:58,502 --> 00:38:59,462
How is it?

546
00:38:59,962 --> 00:39:01,255
It's so yummy!

547
00:39:01,339 --> 00:39:02,173
Do you like it?

548
00:39:04,342 --> 00:39:07,053
When will you come back
from America next time?

549
00:39:08,262 --> 00:39:12,308
Can't you come whenever the boss
goes to church for rice cakes, like today?

550
00:39:12,933 --> 00:39:16,395
My teacher comes on the days
the boss goes to church too.

551
00:39:16,479 --> 00:39:17,438
What?

552
00:39:18,189 --> 00:39:19,023
There he is!

553
00:39:19,690 --> 00:39:21,233
Teacher!

554
00:39:22,360 --> 00:39:26,364
Hey, why didn't you tell me
Kkot-nim had her reading lesson today?

555
00:39:26,447 --> 00:39:27,698
What did you say?

556
00:39:28,783 --> 00:39:30,201
How are you doing, Kkot-nim?

557
00:39:35,581 --> 00:39:38,417
Wait, do you two know each other?

558
00:39:39,543 --> 00:39:40,795
Teacher.

559
00:39:41,629 --> 00:39:43,130
This is great!

560
00:39:43,214 --> 00:39:47,343
Ga-kyung is here, and so is
my hangul teacher!

561
00:39:47,426 --> 00:39:49,136
Hello.

562
00:39:49,762 --> 00:39:50,596
"Ga-kyung"?

563
00:39:52,181 --> 00:39:54,308
So you have hangul lessons today.

564
00:39:54,392 --> 00:39:56,477
If I'd known, I wouldn't have come today.

565
00:39:56,560 --> 00:39:58,104
I should get going.

566
00:39:59,313 --> 00:40:00,272
Kkot-nim.

567
00:40:02,650 --> 00:40:03,609
Ga-kyung?

568
00:40:04,402 --> 00:40:05,528
Is that you, Ga-kyung?

569
00:40:08,239 --> 00:40:09,448
It is you, Lee Ga-kyung!

570
00:40:10,157 --> 00:40:11,409
Didn't you go to America?

571
00:40:12,827 --> 00:40:14,495
Geum-ok, that's--

572
00:40:14,578 --> 00:40:15,788
You rotten thing!

573
00:40:16,372 --> 00:40:17,581
Why are you hitting me?

574
00:40:17,665 --> 00:40:20,292
How could you vanish
without a word, you rotten thing?

575
00:40:20,376 --> 00:40:21,377
You deserve a beating.

576
00:40:21,919 --> 00:40:22,753
That hurts!

577
00:40:23,295 --> 00:40:24,505
Your rubber shoes!

578
00:40:24,588 --> 00:40:25,881
Do they fit okay?

579
00:40:25,965 --> 00:40:28,050
Yeah, they fit perfectly.

580
00:40:28,134 --> 00:40:31,512
Thanks to these,
my toes stay off the dirty ground.

581
00:40:31,595 --> 00:40:33,055
I'm glad. I'd better go now.

582
00:40:33,806 --> 00:40:34,807
Stop distracting me.

583
00:40:34,890 --> 00:40:36,475
- I'm going to whack you with this.
- No.

584
00:40:36,559 --> 00:40:38,185
Wait, stop, that hurts!

585
00:40:38,269 --> 00:40:40,396
- Get over here.
- You'll mess up your shoes!

586
00:40:40,479 --> 00:40:41,313
Hello.

587
00:40:42,398 --> 00:40:43,232
Hello.

588
00:40:44,733 --> 00:40:45,651
Kkot-nim.

589
00:40:46,235 --> 00:40:47,278
What was her name again?

590
00:40:47,361 --> 00:40:51,240
Her name is Lee Ga-kyung. Lee Ga-kyung.

591
00:40:59,331 --> 00:41:00,332
Nice to meet you…

592
00:41:05,796 --> 00:41:06,672
Ms. Lee Ga-kyung.

593
00:41:25,316 --> 00:41:26,150
Oh, right.

594
00:41:26,692 --> 00:41:28,569
I've got another letter from my brother.

595
00:41:33,115 --> 00:41:34,700
IM GEUM-OK

596
00:41:40,414 --> 00:41:41,790
<i>Dear Geum-ok,</i>

597
00:41:42,583 --> 00:41:45,753
<i>my name is Kim Seok-gu.</i>
<i>I've been staying with Geum-deok.</i>

598
00:41:46,712 --> 00:41:49,256
<i>Geum-deok talked about you a lot.</i>

599
00:41:49,340 --> 00:41:50,466
What is it?

600
00:41:51,383 --> 00:41:53,385
Is he asking me to send him money again?

601
00:41:59,558 --> 00:42:02,811
<i>Geum-deok worked hard,</i>
<i>but he got sick from hunger.</i>

602
00:42:03,646 --> 00:42:05,856
<i>The farm wouldn't pay him.</i>

603
00:42:07,233 --> 00:42:10,361
<i>He asked them many times to pay him,</i>
<i>but they beat him instead.</i>

604
00:42:11,153 --> 00:42:12,863
<i>He said he wanted to go home.</i>

605
00:42:14,073 --> 00:42:16,492
<i>Those were his final words.</i>

606
00:42:18,160 --> 00:42:19,119
<i>I'm sorry.</i>

607
00:42:21,622 --> 00:42:22,706
Is that what it says?

608
00:42:23,832 --> 00:42:25,125
That little punk.

609
00:42:25,209 --> 00:42:28,170
He knows I'm barely putting food
on the table by weaving straw mats.

610
00:42:28,254 --> 00:42:30,297
And if he dares ask me for money again…

611
00:42:31,799 --> 00:42:32,716
It says Geum-deok…

612
00:42:35,511 --> 00:42:37,137
gets white rice with every meal.

613
00:42:37,888 --> 00:42:39,807
White rice?

614
00:42:39,890 --> 00:42:40,724
For real?

615
00:42:41,225 --> 00:42:43,852
He left for Manchuria because
we couldn't even afford barley rice.

616
00:42:45,020 --> 00:42:47,481
He's eating well and doing fine.

617
00:42:48,732 --> 00:42:51,527
He says Manchurian winters are brutal,
but it's been warmer lately.

618
00:42:55,864 --> 00:42:58,325
And he says he used the money you sent

619
00:43:00,035 --> 00:43:01,412
to buy himself new clothes.

620
00:43:04,790 --> 00:43:06,125
And this is all I've got.

621
00:43:06,625 --> 00:43:07,960
He's living the good life.

622
00:43:09,878 --> 00:43:10,713
That's not all.

623
00:43:10,796 --> 00:43:13,340
Guess why Geum-deok bought new clothes?

624
00:43:13,424 --> 00:43:14,842
- Why?
- Look here.

625
00:43:14,925 --> 00:43:16,802
He found a girl he likes.

626
00:43:16,885 --> 00:43:17,720
What?

627
00:43:18,304 --> 00:43:19,597
Hey, in that case…

628
00:43:19,680 --> 00:43:20,556
Oh my goodness.

629
00:43:20,639 --> 00:43:22,766
No wonder he needed money.

630
00:43:24,018 --> 00:43:26,103
He's a sharp kid, that one.

631
00:43:28,731 --> 00:43:31,650
Geum-deok says he's going to be
pretty busy from now on.

632
00:43:32,484 --> 00:43:34,528
So even if you don't hear from him much,

633
00:43:35,738 --> 00:43:36,822
don't be concerned.

634
00:43:36,905 --> 00:43:38,991
Who cares about letters now?

635
00:43:39,074 --> 00:43:41,452
My little brother is about to get married.

636
00:43:43,120 --> 00:43:44,496
- That's great.
- Geum-ok.

637
00:43:44,580 --> 00:43:46,415
That's wonderful news.

638
00:43:46,498 --> 00:43:47,499
Tell me about it.

639
00:43:47,583 --> 00:43:49,293
Should I send him some more money?

640
00:44:04,558 --> 00:44:05,768
Are you serious?

641
00:44:09,563 --> 00:44:11,815
You won't listen
unless I do this, will you?

642
00:44:12,566 --> 00:44:14,818
Oh dear.

643
00:44:14,902 --> 00:44:16,570
How could we leave our homes?

644
00:44:16,654 --> 00:44:20,240
What a load of rot!

645
00:44:20,783 --> 00:44:22,451
That's right! And listen.

646
00:44:22,534 --> 00:44:25,162
This is my home.
Where am I supposed to go?

647
00:44:25,245 --> 00:44:27,873
We've sent you
plenty of demolition notices already.

648
00:44:27,956 --> 00:44:28,791
Here, look.

649
00:44:28,874 --> 00:44:30,334
Demolition notices?

650
00:44:30,417 --> 00:44:32,419
We know nothing about that.
What do you mean?

651
00:44:32,503 --> 00:44:34,546
You wouldn't know because you can't read.

652
00:44:34,630 --> 00:44:36,507
Are you telling us to become homeless?

653
00:44:36,590 --> 00:44:39,009
That's your problem
to figure out, damn it!

654
00:44:39,093 --> 00:44:41,762
You wouldn't dare say a word to the Japs!

655
00:44:41,845 --> 00:44:44,306
Why do you come here

656
00:44:44,390 --> 00:44:46,809
and bully your own people? Just why?

657
00:44:46,892 --> 00:44:49,645
You live in a shack next to graves.
What are you complaining about?

658
00:44:49,728 --> 00:44:50,896
What's wrong with the graves?

659
00:44:51,480 --> 00:44:52,690
This is our home!

660
00:44:53,273 --> 00:44:56,443
Where in Joseon isn't there a grave?

661
00:44:56,527 --> 00:45:00,364
People have lived and died on this land
for 5,000 years.

662
00:45:00,948 --> 00:45:02,116
So what?

663
00:45:02,199 --> 00:45:03,951
You call this shack a home?

664
00:45:04,034 --> 00:45:05,577
Even dogs wouldn't live here.

665
00:45:06,787 --> 00:45:08,122
What? Did you just hit me?

666
00:45:08,706 --> 00:45:10,582
You say even dogs wouldn't live here?

667
00:45:11,542 --> 00:45:14,253
The dogs are all living there

668
00:45:14,962 --> 00:45:17,297
at the Government-General.

669
00:45:17,381 --> 00:45:19,383
Those sons of bitches.

670
00:45:20,300 --> 00:45:22,428
Damn Japs, those sons of bitches!

671
00:45:23,303 --> 00:45:26,515
You're nothing but their stooges!

672
00:45:28,726 --> 00:45:30,602
Ruff, ruff! Bark like a dog.

673
00:45:31,478 --> 00:45:32,896
Ruff, ruff!

674
00:45:32,980 --> 00:45:34,148
- Ruff!
- What the hell?

675
00:45:34,231 --> 00:45:35,441
- What's with her?
- Go on, bark.

676
00:45:35,524 --> 00:45:36,900
- Hey, grab her!
- Ruff, ruff!

677
00:45:36,984 --> 00:45:38,402
You crazy bitch!

678
00:45:39,611 --> 00:45:40,863
You scoundrels!

679
00:45:40,946 --> 00:45:42,656
My baby! My baby.

680
00:45:44,032 --> 00:45:44,867
No.

681
00:45:44,950 --> 00:45:46,034
You jerk.

682
00:45:46,118 --> 00:45:47,828
She's seriously insane.

683
00:45:47,911 --> 00:45:49,455
You bastard!

684
00:45:49,538 --> 00:45:50,873
Should I trample you too?

685
00:45:50,956 --> 00:45:51,832
Should I trample you?

686
00:45:56,754 --> 00:45:58,756
Oh no.

687
00:46:01,383 --> 00:46:02,593
You bastard!

688
00:46:03,552 --> 00:46:05,345
You hurt my baby!

689
00:46:05,429 --> 00:46:07,097
- Stop her!
- You!

690
00:46:09,349 --> 00:46:10,726
She's gone mad!

691
00:46:13,812 --> 00:46:17,149
She's unwell. She's not well!

692
00:46:17,232 --> 00:46:18,525
Oh yeah?

693
00:46:18,609 --> 00:46:20,152
I'll make you feel a whole lot worse.

694
00:46:20,235 --> 00:46:21,862
You wait right there.

695
00:46:21,945 --> 00:46:23,906
- Get over here!
- Cut it out!

696
00:46:23,989 --> 00:46:26,617
Who are you telling me to stop,
you crippled bastard!

697
00:46:28,869 --> 00:46:29,745
Mr. Lee!

698
00:46:30,537 --> 00:46:32,164
You bunch of filthy lowlifes.

699
00:46:33,165 --> 00:46:34,416
You all got a death wish?

700
00:46:34,500 --> 00:46:35,459
Are you okay?

701
00:46:36,293 --> 00:46:37,127
Your leg…

702
00:46:37,795 --> 00:46:39,713
How could you beat people up?

703
00:46:39,797 --> 00:46:42,633
You're no better than animals,
you filthy lowlifes!

704
00:46:43,217 --> 00:46:44,092
Geum-ok!

705
00:46:45,719 --> 00:46:46,887
Who are you people?

706
00:46:46,970 --> 00:46:49,056
What gives you the right to barge into--

707
00:46:49,139 --> 00:46:50,599
And who the hell are you?

708
00:46:50,682 --> 00:46:51,767
I said, who are you?

709
00:46:53,018 --> 00:46:53,852
Don't touch her.

710
00:46:55,187 --> 00:46:56,146
Look at this punk.

711
00:46:57,231 --> 00:46:58,148
Let go.

712
00:46:58,232 --> 00:46:59,107
I said let go!

713
00:46:59,191 --> 00:47:00,609
Hands off! You hear me?

714
00:47:03,320 --> 00:47:04,154
Let go!

715
00:47:04,822 --> 00:47:06,073
What? Are you sure?

716
00:47:09,743 --> 00:47:11,537
I'm going to kill you today.

717
00:47:19,127 --> 00:47:20,629
- You bastard--
- Knock it off!

718
00:47:20,712 --> 00:47:21,922
- Stop.
- Let go.

719
00:47:22,005 --> 00:47:23,674
I'll kill that bastard today!

720
00:47:23,757 --> 00:47:27,261
That's Mr. Sato Hideo.
He's going to marry our boss's daughter!

721
00:47:35,435 --> 00:47:36,562
Sato?

722
00:47:53,745 --> 00:47:57,207
I'm still shaken up
over that incident at the banquet.

723
00:47:58,709 --> 00:48:00,502
You weren't hurt, were you?

724
00:48:01,628 --> 00:48:02,462
No, I'm fine.

725
00:48:10,637 --> 00:48:11,471
Oh, right.

726
00:48:11,555 --> 00:48:14,057
What did you think
of the newspaper article?

727
00:48:14,141 --> 00:48:15,517
I arranged it in a hurry.

728
00:48:16,226 --> 00:48:18,228
It was very timely.

729
00:48:18,312 --> 00:48:20,063
I'm glad.

730
00:48:20,147 --> 00:48:22,274
I wanted to do something
for the Government-General…

731
00:48:22,357 --> 00:48:25,527
Or rather, for you, as family.

732
00:48:28,488 --> 00:48:31,783
I arranged the sale
of government property in Gyodang-ri

733
00:48:31,867 --> 00:48:34,703
so I could repay you someday
down the line--

734
00:48:34,786 --> 00:48:36,204
Please keep your voice down.

735
00:48:38,582 --> 00:48:39,875
If there's nothing else,

736
00:48:40,459 --> 00:48:41,668
I'll get going.

737
00:48:45,505 --> 00:48:49,509
I was 16 when I first went into business.

738
00:48:51,553 --> 00:48:53,430
My late father used to say,

739
00:48:54,640 --> 00:48:56,224
"Once a scale tips to one side,

740
00:48:57,142 --> 00:49:00,228
it's bound to tip again.
Don't bother fixing it."

741
00:49:10,572 --> 00:49:13,408
I straightened things out
for you this once.

742
00:49:14,409 --> 00:49:16,244
But if you go off balance again,

743
00:49:17,079 --> 00:49:21,500
then I'll have to invest in a new scale.

744
00:49:36,807 --> 00:49:37,891
We're terribly sorry.

745
00:49:38,392 --> 00:49:41,144
You're Mr. Sato Hideo,
the son of Inspector General Sato

746
00:49:41,228 --> 00:49:43,188
at the Government-General, right?

747
00:49:46,608 --> 00:49:47,776
The Government-General?

748
00:49:48,276 --> 00:49:50,404
We work for Joil Textile Factory.

749
00:49:50,487 --> 00:49:53,198
Please forgive our rudeness.

750
00:49:53,907 --> 00:49:54,825
Hey.

751
00:49:54,908 --> 00:49:56,576
My apologies, Mr. Sato Hideo.

752
00:49:56,660 --> 00:49:57,703
Please forgive me!

753
00:49:58,912 --> 00:50:00,622
I'm not the one who deserves your apology.

754
00:50:01,665 --> 00:50:02,541
Pardon?

755
00:50:03,125 --> 00:50:04,459
Yes, sir.

756
00:50:04,543 --> 00:50:06,628
We're terribly sorry. Please forgive us.

757
00:50:06,712 --> 00:50:08,130
What a cutie.

758
00:50:09,172 --> 00:50:11,174
We were only here on our boss's orders

759
00:50:11,258 --> 00:50:13,260
to notify the residents
about the relocation.

760
00:50:13,343 --> 00:50:14,594
We meant nothing else by it.

761
00:50:15,262 --> 00:50:16,722
- You should leave now.
- Sorry?

762
00:50:17,723 --> 00:50:19,725
Yes, sir. Come on, hurry.

763
00:50:21,643 --> 00:50:23,228
They're leaving just like that?

764
00:50:29,526 --> 00:50:31,403
Good grief, the teacher was…

765
00:50:31,486 --> 00:50:32,738
Kkot-nim's teacher…

766
00:50:33,905 --> 00:50:34,740
I'm sorry.

767
00:50:38,452 --> 00:50:39,286
I'm sorry.

768
00:50:48,045 --> 00:50:48,879
Is it true?

769
00:50:55,886 --> 00:50:57,763
Tell me. Is it really true?

770
00:50:59,514 --> 00:51:00,932
Why aren't you answering me?

771
00:51:05,479 --> 00:51:07,481
Are you really

772
00:51:07,564 --> 00:51:10,025
the son of someone important
at the Government-General?

773
00:51:11,485 --> 00:51:13,904
And you're marrying
into the Joil Textile family?

774
00:51:16,698 --> 00:51:17,824
What's your deal?

775
00:51:18,658 --> 00:51:21,453
Was teaching Kkot-nim to read
all part of some scheme?

776
00:51:21,536 --> 00:51:22,370
What for?

777
00:51:23,038 --> 00:51:25,916
What are you planning to do here
after driving us all out?

778
00:51:26,583 --> 00:51:28,835
Do we look like a joke to you?

779
00:51:32,923 --> 00:51:34,091
That's enough, No-ma.

780
00:51:35,801 --> 00:51:36,635
What?

781
00:51:39,763 --> 00:51:42,182
Things aren't always what they seem.

782
00:51:42,265 --> 00:51:45,018
So don't judge
when you don't know the whole story.

783
00:51:45,102 --> 00:51:46,478
Are you

784
00:51:47,062 --> 00:51:48,230
taking his side now?

785
00:51:49,314 --> 00:51:50,524
No, that's not it.

786
00:51:51,066 --> 00:51:51,900
Ga-kyung!

787
00:51:53,401 --> 00:51:55,445
Since you work with Japs
at the Government-General,

788
00:51:55,529 --> 00:51:56,863
do you think you're Japanese now?

789
00:51:57,489 --> 00:51:59,449
Do you even know who he is?

790
00:51:59,533 --> 00:52:02,369
His father is the leader of
the Jap bastards who stole our country

791
00:52:02,452 --> 00:52:05,372
and his father-in-law is a traitor
who's trying to drive us out.

792
00:52:06,373 --> 00:52:08,208
No-ma. Just hear me out.

793
00:52:10,293 --> 00:52:11,920
Oh, I see. You…

794
00:52:12,504 --> 00:52:14,131
Don't tell me you like this guy.

795
00:52:28,311 --> 00:52:29,146
So it's true.

796
00:52:30,647 --> 00:52:34,192
First you serve the Japs like a dog,
and now you're messing around with one?

797
00:52:34,276 --> 00:52:35,235
Just like your mom?

798
00:52:56,256 --> 00:52:57,090
Ga-kyung.

799
00:52:57,883 --> 00:52:59,134
That's not what I meant.

800
00:52:59,217 --> 00:53:00,218
You're right.

801
00:53:01,845 --> 00:53:04,222
I'm the daughter of a woman
who slept with a Japanese man.

802
00:53:07,767 --> 00:53:08,602
Ga-kyung.

803
00:53:44,387 --> 00:53:45,222
I'm sorry.

804
00:53:45,305 --> 00:53:46,556
You seem to have a fever.

805
00:53:47,182 --> 00:53:49,434
You may have overdone it going out today.

806
00:53:50,810 --> 00:53:51,728
Get some rest.

807
00:53:52,979 --> 00:53:53,897
I want Seo-hee…

808
00:53:55,857 --> 00:53:58,652
to go to America, not Shanghai.

809
00:53:59,611 --> 00:54:01,404
I want to keep my word to her.

810
00:54:01,488 --> 00:54:02,656
Yes, I understand.

811
00:54:03,949 --> 00:54:06,701
But I can't guarantee it.

812
00:54:06,785 --> 00:54:07,786
I'll do what I can.

813
00:54:07,869 --> 00:54:09,162
I know you can.

814
00:54:12,958 --> 00:54:14,334
Please marry her.

815
00:54:17,045 --> 00:54:20,173
Just as you married me
and got me an American passport,

816
00:54:21,174 --> 00:54:22,717
I want you to marry her

817
00:54:22,801 --> 00:54:25,679
and get her an American passport
so she can go to America.

818
00:54:27,639 --> 00:54:28,473
So-ran.

819
00:54:28,556 --> 00:54:30,600
Once I kill Sato,

820
00:54:32,352 --> 00:54:35,355
I won't need an American passport anymore.

821
00:54:35,438 --> 00:54:36,273
So-ran.

822
00:54:55,834 --> 00:54:56,835
Listen, So-ran.

823
00:55:00,046 --> 00:55:02,924
I do think there's a chance
she turned on us.

824
00:55:03,008 --> 00:55:05,969
That's what being a spy does.
It eats you up inside.

825
00:55:06,052 --> 00:55:08,179
Still, I suggested we send her to Shanghai

826
00:55:08,263 --> 00:55:10,932
because you worry about her
and care about her.

827
00:55:11,016 --> 00:55:12,392
It is all for you.

828
00:55:14,769 --> 00:55:16,229
Now you want me to marry her?

829
00:55:23,945 --> 00:55:27,282
I married you because you're you, So-ran.
Because it was you.

830
00:55:29,326 --> 00:55:31,244
And now you're telling me…

831
00:55:33,747 --> 00:55:36,333
you're going to die
right before my very eyes.

832
00:55:48,887 --> 00:55:53,141
Watching you run a high fever all night,
I was terrified…

833
00:55:58,646 --> 00:55:59,522
I'd lose you.

834
00:57:19,144 --> 00:57:20,687
So she's not

835
00:57:21,187 --> 00:57:22,313
Keum Seok-jung's wife?

836
00:57:22,397 --> 00:57:23,231
No, sir.

837
00:57:23,731 --> 00:57:25,942
We questioned everyone in Kyeam-ri.

838
00:57:26,734 --> 00:57:28,153
- Is this woman
- Is this woman

839
00:57:28,236 --> 00:57:30,572
- Keum Seok-jung's wife?
- Keum Seok-jung's wife?

840
00:57:32,949 --> 00:57:34,075
They say it's not her.

841
00:57:39,164 --> 00:57:41,875
<i>Every single one said</i>
<i>she wasn't Keum Seok-jung's wife.</i>

842
00:57:43,084 --> 00:57:44,169
<i>But</i>

843
00:57:44,252 --> 00:57:47,005
I think we finally got a lead on them.

844
00:57:49,090 --> 00:57:51,718
The name of Keum Seok-jung's
dead daughter was…

845
00:57:51,801 --> 00:57:53,261
No, it's not her.

846
00:57:53,344 --> 00:57:55,054
Seo-hee's mom was ugly.

847
00:57:55,138 --> 00:57:56,639
She wasn't this pretty.

848
00:57:57,140 --> 00:57:59,934
She said Seo-hee's mom
didn't look this beautiful.

849
00:58:01,102 --> 00:58:02,145
<i>…Seo-hee.</i>

850
00:58:02,770 --> 00:58:05,023
<i>Keum Seo-hee. That was her name.</i>

851
00:58:05,899 --> 00:58:07,192
Keum Seo-hee.

852
00:58:10,111 --> 00:58:10,987
Yes.

853
00:58:13,114 --> 00:58:15,116
Dinner at your residence tomorrow?

854
00:58:16,075 --> 00:58:16,951
That's right.

855
00:58:17,619 --> 00:58:19,370
I'd like you to bring your wife.

856
00:58:53,279 --> 00:58:54,113
We're here.

857
00:58:55,031 --> 00:58:57,659
Yes, we are.

858
00:59:07,460 --> 00:59:08,461
Well…

859
00:59:09,837 --> 00:59:11,464
About what No-ma said,

860
00:59:12,048 --> 00:59:12,882
I hope…

861
00:59:14,968 --> 00:59:17,554
you don't take it too hard.

862
00:59:19,889 --> 00:59:20,723
All right.

863
00:59:27,689 --> 00:59:29,148
Don't worry about Gyodang-ri.

864
00:59:29,649 --> 00:59:31,526
I'll try to persuade Mr. Kim.

865
00:59:32,986 --> 00:59:36,698
Why would you… do that?

866
00:59:45,832 --> 00:59:47,333
Can you trust me?

867
00:59:51,379 --> 00:59:54,382
No matter what I do or say?

868
00:59:56,801 --> 00:59:59,178
You don't have to answer me right now.

869
01:00:00,096 --> 01:00:00,930
But…

870
01:00:03,975 --> 01:00:04,934
for now…

871
01:00:06,603 --> 01:00:08,021
I just want you to trust me.

872
01:00:16,571 --> 01:00:17,405
Lee Ga-kyung.

873
01:00:17,488 --> 01:00:18,323
Yes?

874
01:00:22,076 --> 01:00:23,202
I mean…

875
01:00:23,286 --> 01:00:24,621
I wasn't answering you.

876
01:00:24,704 --> 01:00:26,205
Lee Ga-kyung suits you…

877
01:00:29,334 --> 01:00:31,044
much better than Keum Seo-hee.

878
01:00:39,344 --> 01:00:41,262
Lee Ga-kyung.

879
01:00:46,267 --> 01:00:48,019
May I call you that?

880
01:00:54,442 --> 01:00:56,235
I do want an answer to that now.

881
01:01:00,448 --> 01:01:01,366
<i>Mom.</i>

882
01:01:09,749 --> 01:01:11,459
<i>When I'm with him,</i>

883
01:01:13,211 --> 01:01:14,837
<i>I don't want to be Keum Seo-hee.</i>

884
01:01:15,546 --> 01:01:18,216
<i>I want to be Lee Ga-kyung.</i>

885
01:01:20,677 --> 01:01:22,095
<i>Without deception.</i>

886
01:01:23,096 --> 01:01:23,930
<i>Just…</i>

887
01:01:24,681 --> 01:01:25,682
<i>as myself.</i>

888
01:01:30,853 --> 01:01:34,065
<i>But I'm a spy.</i>

889
01:01:35,149 --> 01:01:36,818
<i>I shouldn't be doing this.</i>

890
01:01:39,445 --> 01:01:40,321
Yes…

891
01:01:43,908 --> 01:01:46,619
Interpreter Kim Tae-ung.

892
01:02:01,968 --> 01:02:04,262
<i>It's too late to go back.</i>

893
01:02:05,763 --> 01:02:07,056
<i>What do I do?</i>

894
01:02:07,932 --> 01:02:08,850
<i>Mom.</i>

895
01:02:50,850 --> 01:02:53,478
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

896
01:02:53,561 --> 01:02:55,188
<i>This has to be a trap.</i>

897
01:02:55,271 --> 01:02:58,483
<i>Do you want us</i>
<i>to arrest Keum Seo-hee tonight?</i>

898
01:02:58,566 --> 01:03:00,234
<i>We have no more time to waste.</i>

899
01:03:00,318 --> 01:03:01,819
<i>We need to do whatever we can.</i>

900
01:03:01,903 --> 01:03:03,112
<i>This pneumatic tube thing</i>

901
01:03:03,196 --> 01:03:05,740
is more than big enough to fit a bomb.

902
01:03:05,823 --> 01:03:08,409
<i>We could get a bomb into their hands</i>
<i>in two and a half minutes?</i>

903
01:03:09,076 --> 01:03:10,661
<i>Didn't I just tell you?</i>

904
01:03:10,745 --> 01:03:12,121
<i>No one leaves the building.</i>

905
01:03:12,205 --> 01:03:13,748
I said I had something prepared.

906
01:03:16,793 --> 01:03:17,627
I think Keum Seo-hee

907
01:03:19,378 --> 01:03:20,880
<i>is a spy.</i>

908
01:03:30,932 --> 01:03:32,934
Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim
